### 1. secret_t2_args_write_file_fail.oo
```
// SECRET: token
pub fn main(fs: &FsCap) {
    let token = "s3cret";
    let path = "/tmp/ooda_t2_args_should_not_write";
    let r = write_file(fs, path, token);
    println(1);
}
```
Live: refuse content arg (argi=2) via `c_secret_scan_call(..., want=2, eq_only=true)`.

### 2. secret_t2_args_tcp_write_fail.oo
```
// SECRET: token
pub fn main(net: &NetCap) {
    let token = "s3cret-payload";
    let slot = 0;
    let r = tcp_write(net, slot, token);
    println(1);
}
```
Live: refuse payload (argi>=1) via net sink branch in `check_secret_println_sinks`
(`tcp_write` / `net_tcp_write` / `net_send` / `http_post` / …). Emit-time
`c_secret_emit_guard` also scans a1/a2 + full `args` via `c_guard_scan_idents`.

### 3. secret_t2_args_println_secret_fail.oo
```
// SECRET: token
pub fn main() {
    let token = "s3cret";
    println(token);
}
```
Live: bare IDENT walk in println args → refuse.

### 4. secret_t2_args_println_concat_fail.oo
```
// SECRET: token
pub fn main() {
    let token = "s3cret";
    println("literal " + token);
}
```
Live: println arg walker visits every IDENT (not bare-only), so `token` inside
`"literal " + token` is refused. This is the T2-class concat-in-args case
(string literal alone is not a secret).

### 5. secret_t2_args_println_ok_pass.oo
```
// SECRET: other
pub fn main() {
    let other = "s3cret-not-printed";
    println("no secret");
}
```
Live: string-literal-only println does not touch SECRET-tagged `other` → OK.
Proves tag presence alone does not poison unrelated clean sinks.

